\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x - y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})=- x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x - y)^2+(- x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(x (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2}) - y (- x^{2} - x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x - y)^{2} + (- x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} + 3 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y + 4 x y^{4} + 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135b727b-90a5-58f3-b28d-73bc883b71d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530663} {:answer/id #uuid "1b855307-7285-5bec-af0e-c68f5677735c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530668} {:answer/id #uuid "6d953e63-3844-5dda-bebb-b265d30d5e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2}} (2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530686} {:answer/id #uuid "7897076e-28dc-5d0e-b8bb-01136fb78143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) ((- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530693} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cf50ea-4319-5a4f-ba19-0ba8b226670e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}}{\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "135b727b-90a5-58f3-b28d-73bc883b71d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530663} :answer-field/id #uuid "172c374d-031f-5a6c-9d2b-39412dfe32ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f47f306f-a5eb-5b1d-8c05-eca24441fbf6" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 4 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 4 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(2 x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y^{2}) \\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534404} {:answer/id #uuid "54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534433} {:answer/id #uuid "5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534457} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} :answer-field/id #uuid "a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161328" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8726e024-292d-59b4-8013-fd7bf0f67e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+e)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534558} {:answer/id #uuid "c5558c16-5207-5627-b751-3fcb910575e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((2+e)^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534707} {:answer/id #uuid "c66cb474-bc45-59be-8448-dccc9423f5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((2+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534710} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534726} {:answer/id #uuid "d59e1bf3-fe94-540a-b645-1d3ca9e31091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+e)^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0a3efb-4ed4-5af7-8085-fc0e737a543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534726} :answer-field/id #uuid "de37fba6-41ca-540b-a8fd-47ec845611d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161354" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $x\\le y\\le x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}y]}_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}(1+{e}^{2x})^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f64d453-abc2-52b8-9d32-f5007c10820a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{5}(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534329} {:answer/id #uuid "22931441-e80d-5d69-b5da-e45afb70211d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534340} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534353} {:answer/id #uuid "3809e723-7c0b-5a79-a57f-42ba07e98577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534373} {:answer/id #uuid "90b9897e-bd16-5166-a704-48996320daaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aa47af8-9c69-50d1-901d-f11e4c5ee62b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534353} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d007d0a-e619-522c-9617-4f4a41fec80b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161350" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-3x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $3x\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-3x)=-3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-3x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{1+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{3x}^{3}\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(y-3x)^{3/2}]}_{y=3x}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-3x)^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\,{[\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(3-3x)^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(0-{3}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228ec946-8873-5f8b-8a2a-49f36370152e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534338} {:answer/id #uuid "9877fc34-f311-559c-8cc0-f65810b60958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534605} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ff7c33-ae77-5848-9080-a0accbff86b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2y+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534631} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f1d54-0fa6-57df-9370-78329241cb08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}y+4{y}^{2}+\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534699} {:answer/id #uuid "ea232514-d5a5-5e3b-af1e-b89e96005805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9877fc34-f311-559c-8cc0-f65810b60958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534605} :answer-field/id #uuid "1808b286-0193-5bc8-a2c6-690d66c8a263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f409435d-6032-5802-bf1f-c20613731f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161327" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(x+\\sqrt{2}y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1071da23-196f-5794-9ffa-3f740ac06877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549197} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0e5d04-50bb-58f3-b905-0c199719febe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549204} {:answer/id #uuid "9d38236d-d731-58c6-aab4-26a66cc6fad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549214} {:answer/id #uuid "9de8191b-6472-5369-91cc-2fa36ced68db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549215} {:answer/id #uuid "d34d113a-4224-5ee8-a283-8b0d1abca990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d38236d-d731-58c6-aab4-26a66cc6fad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549214} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c6f3a9-02d6-5bc3-9868-9809e2ff90ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0fa7fa7e-c30f-5aad-b305-af6fc97a49df" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + 2 y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =9{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0051c0dc-b140-5c37-b91b-fa0644aff402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549195} {:answer/id #uuid "43d55685-dedc-5ead-b19e-09ba1758d7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549205} {:answer/id #uuid "5837e7b0-c6cc-5ef7-b5ab-4f00a8ca70ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549207} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d58e5e-bcd9-5dd5-8ccc-83603c830e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549217} {:answer/id #uuid "b821fabe-ac0b-5414-a4e2-8b1ed4243abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0051c0dc-b140-5c37-b91b-fa0644aff402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549195} :answer-field/id #uuid "da6b5da1-113b-5ce0-ba81-9a7204fceb78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "161eeef3-7763-576b-af4d-53c8662ff260" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + y^{2} + 1$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-2\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2} + 1)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y^{2} + 1)=2 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\left(x + y^{2} + 1\\right)-x+y^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(2 y\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{4 y^{2} + 2}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{2\\left(2 y^{2} + 1\\right)}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}}\\sqrt{2}\\cancel{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}4 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(-2\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263bec6f-8fab-5a99-bce0-3bd6e2ee6c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549201} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4b6d7a-30b0-5938-825b-8aa9bd3f0580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549218} {:answer/id #uuid "c43b536e-4696-5803-a535-e7756f41d100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549221} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bd8410-2775-5436-81af-14aad8107cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549228} {:answer/id #uuid "fa631a1e-b650-5899-ab31-004c8a3b3cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8bd8410-2775-5436-81af-14aad8107cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549228} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ab3e89-4254-53dc-8788-c69f6a8e3476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70de3dca-c539-516f-9ac7-21707e474aee" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + 2 y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + 2 y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3 \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\left(1-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6 \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b260818-1060-55fa-8c8b-f9e91ebdcca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549203} {:answer/id #uuid "43ecf632-007a-5928-a792-8b29d247dcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549206} {:answer/id #uuid "8acb9345-b59e-5eab-a262-c23a2ff357c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549209} {:answer/id #uuid "9a03bcbf-d915-5fc2-9646-850a04739d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549212} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6848e4-99c2-58d9-b9c8-52927b9e5d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6848e4-99c2-58d9-b9c8-52927b9e5d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549213} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bae13c6-8cb9-596f-beee-05fbf444040a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "940d874d-7f17-585c-aaa1-3e37ae6a49cf" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =2 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2455013a-1ab6-5685-a102-5887f7847254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549200} {:answer/id #uuid "2918a66a-cecc-5ac0-ad60-13877418924c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549202} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b8226-2d14-5ab6-a10c-36050a0a6b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549216} {:answer/id #uuid "c467c12b-a89b-55c5-bf6f-5be1e6953f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549222} {:answer/id #uuid "da9c1189-a115-5218-bb70-bb9e877dca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9c1189-a115-5218-bb70-bb9e877dca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549226} :answer-field/id #uuid "efca1546-0a41-50f6-8cf2-c2773894182b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d486a075-a82c-5df5-890f-0518c34dd229" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + 2 y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559996} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6f9c08-fc7c-5686-90ec-bd5e19971270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560003} {:answer/id #uuid "9107f827-2e1b-5ff9-a378-2a6c0345e47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560023} {:answer/id #uuid "bf504992-846a-53c0-b739-ef1c87c02b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560036} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ff7344-e51a-5da3-99bd-f7f28794aacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d783c00-550c-539f-9270-98d59cd31448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "141fe36a-507a-57e3-9528-7652085c824c" :question/problem "![](images/01/013cdd1ba73ee79ef51d00b7dc9f48488cee2299eb593accadfbf026981169c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{32 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5106df-61b9-5b24-863c-2ae5e567f591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559989} {:answer/id #uuid "2417f5d3-5389-5044-b2a0-418aeb7dac86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559997} {:answer/id #uuid "58f3e230-b70f-57b4-82bf-17794d911a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560008} {:answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560009} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa96423-8207-5f28-adff-de3f6375c04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560009} :answer-field/id #uuid "92fd1ae2-283e-5689-8e46-7ea5679c7ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c380e3c-eb26-50bd-a669-b181f14f84c3" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09a881e6809e097e982b58d81a037d162f6b5820894e172955df1551415ca.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cdfb2d-5fc7-5150-b284-439e38260d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559994} {:answer/id #uuid "4676e0a2-75d8-5a17-a969-7c99e3fa2152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560004} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac954c9-a0ef-5924-b0d7-d5aac29af9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560018} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560049} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db0de2-0b22-5269-b5c3-7a0ea98a8477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560049} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9f6651-4921-50c2-a3b2-e6a39b3ee3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d921d71-b4a3-5244-8d78-9db05b10437e" :question/problem "![](images/13/137a452f1b378359fdd974b11781dddf1e7e9b469d864699b044eaf1cc1a7c20.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc3cc91-3f29-59d8-80b6-0509e2fa9b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559991} {:answer/id #uuid "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559999} {:answer/id #uuid "32785a38-9413-5c2f-86f3-80004ad4a202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560001} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d0ede3-115f-5b05-9411-95243502c7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560047} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a86298-3ae4-55b4-9fd8-1c22396d65d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559999} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e408e6-0de3-55b6-a2e4-474fb0066fcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ef90de0-c8fe-5f06-94ae-fa8b5651011c" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af59f24-43d6-5c12-b0e9-e9ef1fc3b437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559988} {:answer/id #uuid "78db087d-54a3-5074-9702-51a2dfed6bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560016} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d9c1d2-25db-5084-ac89-6a69bec1929c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560027} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d7b1f1-76c5-5f4b-906b-3b95fe95622c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560032} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560046} :answer-field/id #uuid "65cba47d-19ee-5caf-9052-3301717925d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79638187-bf06-5eeb-9e92-84390aacec40" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb0f77c-0203-5719-ab9d-91ce8ca040f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559995} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560026} {:answer/id #uuid "b35d874d-13e5-52c0-93cc-3d0c92d94055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560033} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4b7fd6-e628-5a99-898f-4ef27ca7dfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560040} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf8fd0-a189-5a52-a852-f75ff62ece6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560026} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e19b400-81aa-5f00-9d55-be29eb1d866d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "873a8289-cea9-556f-b99b-1f305a18b7fd" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =\\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0319e11a-534e-5e66-aa6f-c12e5f170d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559986} {:answer/id #uuid "069dbf70-7416-5aa9-b93b-1c0d75ec550f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559987} {:answer/id #uuid "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560014} {:answer/id #uuid "d1731e24-69f6-5cbf-9e13-2f9603b51d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560042} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0bb7a-9649-5c57-b6ec-ac42c2d42781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560014} :answer-field/id #uuid "779704ff-9be7-5e93-805f-a309e7bb251f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8aec1038-bbbf-5f1f-8cad-aecf57bf997f" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315f5b77-c3b2-50de-ab4c-837f464ef698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560000} {:answer/id #uuid "a55d1153-0d00-5bef-9de8-4db03c5e4271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560029} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f52bae-425e-5b13-a313-db368e2739f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560030} {:answer/id #uuid "b570d8ce-b35e-58a9-a28f-8963dd0ebe8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560034} {:answer/id #uuid "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560043} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9e3b15-fafe-5f5f-9850-3350e5bcb68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a43799a6-bf36-5f02-8250-1856789bacb5" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{3 r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((6))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f81263-c580-52d1-90e1-7fa8854069df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560010} {:answer/id #uuid "654cc51a-56c2-5bce-942c-7269e2a14dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560011} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb4f72-b6e7-5245-ba1e-f747a1cb18d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560022} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3dcf5-5100-544e-ae55-56e98c4a35fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560041} {:answer/id #uuid "fe74912e-c4ea-527e-af0a-bd1df787f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3dcf5-5100-544e-ae55-56e98c4a35fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560041} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a717221-6128-50c0-937b-dab9d64bde09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2b56f89-2065-56e0-ba13-8da185fac240" :question/problem "![](images/81/819f039dfc5397bf393de37bbb17f4cb8888ce53a5370bfdf13d172697ffd2f7.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c882a41-9fb5-5aa6-bfe4-ddedb1e09450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559990} {:answer/id #uuid "38dc5a67-0af1-5b0c-9479-a3e6537981d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560002} {:answer/id #uuid "c282306c-0f8e-565e-a571-f8ea4d9770bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560037} {:answer/id #uuid "e291533d-3639-5a90-afba-d6eb272eb268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(-2 + \\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560044} {:answer/id #uuid "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560054} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56e79b1-49d0-59b4-9498-4712b7f8d4c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3db0b63-f6f4-5b4a-a9c1-b1b2cfd6f6dd" :question/problem "![](images/44/44fada7635b47f3f7ef635b0466f21d87c71b52e7f39fddb5d36c16472f4ce82.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the second quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[r^{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((0))-((1))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((0)-(1)) \\\\ & =- 8 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $- 8 \\sqrt{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486a4156-dc1b-5b7b-9229-38afb6b5e2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6x+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600562} {:answer/id #uuid "920085e0-2d08-56fc-82c5-43e611d3ca04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600621} {:answer/id #uuid "9fff8871-ae1e-5ba5-a3d9-9c730e8f3057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6xy-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600629} {:answer/id #uuid "d3616013-3ef5-5c69-9b16-7b466745b63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6xy-6{y}^{2})\\sqrt{38x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600666} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ea7308-35d5-50fc-935b-eff909891338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6xy+{y}^{2}x)\\sqrt{36x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fff8871-ae1e-5ba5-a3d9-9c730e8f3057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6xy-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{38}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600629} :answer-field/id #uuid "6514a108-0bfa-5736-b0f3-8a232742dcaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "40236e4a-61b0-58be-b344-bc568186f4e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161324" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=-zy,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-6x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6x+y)=-6,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-6)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,y(6x-y)\\sqrt{38}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,(6xy-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{38}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60cf133a-84d4-5835-a4ca-925a613a1b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600582} {:answer/id #uuid "81adf5c1-53ae-5feb-8d44-e8d7de1ec450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600607} {:answer/id #uuid "b3577a9b-7c5f-5ce2-aa22-4d25f2490ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600640} {:answer/id #uuid "d8bad009-76fe-5d91-a294-a131a16274ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600671} {:answer/id #uuid "fe271613-1293-57c4-b887-6a2841a85a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe271613-1293-57c4-b887-6a2841a85a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600705} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c2d47bd-967a-5b22-a363-5c01f2492eff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab516887-878f-59b1-a540-faf5f487ccc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161317" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}y,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-2x-2y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x-2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x-2y)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2)}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,12{x}^{2}y\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
